Yes. The interesting engine here is not “primitive people misunderstand technology.” It is that every society explains unfamiliar power using the conceptual vocabulary it already has. Cage gets dropped inside that process while it is happening—and eventually realizes modern people do exactly the same thing with things they cannot yet explain.

Nicolas Cage first appeared to the people of Akwena by falling out of the sky.

This was unfortunate.

Not because he fell very far.

He landed in a banana grove.

It was unfortunate because, approximately thirty seconds earlier, an airplane had crossed overhead.

The people of Akwena had never seen one.

They had heard it first.

A low thunder rolling across a perfectly blue morning.

Children ran from their homes.

Hunters looked upward.

Birds scattered from the canopy.

Then the silver object appeared above the trees, impossibly large, impossibly fast, leaving a white scar across the heavens.

Everyone watched until it vanished.

Nobody spoke.

Then Nicolas Cage crashed through a banana plant.

There was a long silence.

Cage slowly sat up.

Leaves covered his head.

His sunglasses were crooked.

A chicken stood beside him.

He looked around at approximately forty people holding spears.

“Hello.”

Nobody moved.

Cage looked upward.

The airplane was gone.

He looked back at the villagers.

“Oh.”

An elderly man whispered something.

Several people gasped.

Cage pointed toward himself.

“No.”

They stared.

“No, no, no.”

The elder stepped forward.

Cage pointed upward.

“Plane.”

Then at himself.

“Nicolas Cage.”

The elder looked upward.

Then at Cage.

Then upward again.

Cage saw the connection being constructed in real time.

“No.”

The elder fell to his knees.

Cage closed his eyes.

“Absolutely not.”

—

The people called the airplane The Silver Ancestor.

They called Cage The Man Who Fell Behind It.

Cage spent most of his first day objecting.

“I did not come from the Silver Ancestor.”

A translator eventually emerged: a young trader named Kito who spoke enough English to make everything slightly worse.

Kito translated Cage’s statement.

The village elders discussed it.

Then Kito turned back.

“They say this is expected.”

“What is expected?”

“That messengers deny being messengers.”

Cage stared.

“Why?”

“Humility.”

“That is extremely inconvenient.”

Kito nodded sympathetically.

“They admire you.”

Cage pointed upward.

“That was an airplane.”

Kito repeated the word carefully.

“Air… plane.”

“Yes.”

“What is it?”

“A machine.”

Kito translated.

The elders murmured.

“What did you tell them?”

“Machine.”

“What does machine mean in your language?”

Kito thought.

“A thing given motion through captured intention.”

Cage paused.

“That is actually pretty good.”

The elders murmured louder.

Cage immediately regretted saying this.

—

Over the following week, Nicolas Cage attempted to explain aviation.

This produced a religion.

He drew an airplane in the dirt.

Wings.

Fuselage.

Engines.

One elder studied it carefully.

“So the Silver Ancestor has two fire-hearts.”

“No. Engines.”

“Fire-hearts.”

“Engines.”

Kito translated.

“Things that drink dead earth and produce wind.”

Cage stared at him.

“What did you just say?”

“That is what fuel means.”

“No, it isn’t.”

“It is close.”

“It is alarmingly poetic, but it is not close.”

The elder nodded solemnly.

“The Ancestor consumes the buried blood of the world.”

Cage rubbed his face.

“Jet fuel.”

Kito shrugged.

“We do not have jet fuel.”

“Yes. I am noticing the problem.”

—

Then they discovered his flashlight.

Cage had been carrying one in his jacket.

A child found it.

Pressed the button.

Light appeared.

At noon.

The child screamed.

Everyone screamed.

Cage screamed because everyone else screamed.

Eventually the village gathered around it.

Cage held up the flashlight.

“This is electricity.”

Kito translated.

“What did you say?”

“Lightning sleeping inside stone.”

Cage stared.

“It is a battery.”

Kito nodded.

“Lightning sleeping inside a smaller stone.”

Cage looked at the villagers.

They looked at him.

He slowly put the flashlight away.

“We are going to stop explaining things.”

—

That did not help.

Because now the villagers began observing Cage.

Everything he did became meaningful.

He checked his wristwatch.

The elders concluded he could consult invisible cycles.

He used a lighter.

They decided he carried captive sun.

He opened a granola bar.

The wrapper crinkled.

A child whispered:

“Thunder food.”

Cage said:

“No.”

The name stayed.

Worst of all was his phone.

It had no signal.

But it still contained photographs.

Cage showed Kito a picture of New York.

Buildings.

Cars.

Lights.

Thousands of windows.

Kito stared for almost a minute.

Then:

“Your people live inside mountains.”

“No.”

“Those are not mountains?”

“Buildings.”

“What is a building?”

Cage opened his mouth.

Stopped.

Closed it.

“Artificial mountains.”

Kito smiled.

Cage pointed.

“Do not enjoy this.”

—

Months passed.

Cage remained stranded.

He became less interested in correcting the Akwena and more interested in understanding how they were constructing meaning.

Their mythology grew.

Not randomly.

That surprised him.

The airplane became associated with thunder because it sounded like thunder.

Its contrail became associated with sky-serpents because it resembled the pale seasonal snakes that emerged after rain.

The pilots became unseen navigators because nobody could see people inside the machine.

The aircraft’s disappearance over the horizon became a journey into the ancestral west, where the sun also disappeared.

Cage realized something.

They were not merely making things up.

They were performing inference with insufficient information.

Pattern.

Analogy.

Compression.

Existing category.

New phenomenon.

Fit the unfamiliar into the closest available model.

Cage sat beside Kito one evening.

“So the Silver Ancestor mythology…”

Kito looked offended.

“What mythology?”

Cage caught himself.

“The Silver Ancestor teachings.”

“Yes.”

“They came from things people already knew.”

“Of course.”

“Thunder. Birds. Fire. Ancestors.”

“Yes.”

Cage looked toward the darkening sky.

“You weren’t explaining the airplane.”

Kito frowned.

“We were.”

“No.”

Cage smiled.

“You were explaining your experience of the airplane.”

Kito thought about this.

“And you are different?”

Cage looked over.

“What?”

“When you saw our rain ceremony, you called it weather prediction.”

“Yes.”

“When you saw the healer use bark, you called it medicine.”

“Yes.”

“When hunters watched birds to find water, you called it ecology.”

Cage went quiet.

Kito smiled.

“You also rename things so they fit inside your world.”

Cage stared ahead for a long time.

“Damn.”

Kito nodded.

“I believe that means I am correct.”

—

Eventually another airplane came.

The entire village heard it.

People poured into the clearing.

Cage sprinted outside.

“YES!”

Everyone stared upward.

This aircraft was lower.

A small twin-engine survey plane.

Cage began waving.

The villagers did not.

They knelt.

Cage turned.

“No! Stand up!”

Nobody moved.

“This is not—”

The aircraft circled.

Cage ripped off his jacket and waved it.

The plane circled again.

Then disappeared beyond the ridge.

The village erupted.

People embraced.

Children danced.

Drums sounded.

Cage stood frozen.

Kito approached.

“The Silver Ancestor returned for you.”

“No.”

“It saw you.”

“It was probably a survey aircraft.”

“It circled twice.”

“They saw movement.”

“It answered.”

“No.”

“You waved.”

“Yes.”

“It turned.”

“Yes.”

Kito smiled.

“You communicated.”

Cage opened his mouth.

Nothing came out.

“Technically,” he finally admitted, “that is difficult to argue with.”

—

Two days later, outsiders arrived.

Six researchers.

Radios.

Backpacks.

GPS units.

A satellite phone.

Medical kits.

They had seen Cage from the aircraft and returned with a ground team.

The villagers watched them emerge from the forest.

One researcher spoke into a radio.

A distant voice answered.

Half the village gasped.

Kito whispered:

“He speaks to absent people.”

Cage started to correct him.

Then stopped.

Because that was exactly what was happening.

Another researcher unfolded a solar panel.

The villagers watched sunlight become stored electricity.

A woman used a GPS receiver to determine their position.

A medic examined a child’s infected cut and produced antibiotics.

Cage watched the mythology accelerating.

Sky maps.

Talking stones.

Sun cloth.

Healing powder.

Invisible voices.

He finally understood the real problem.

It wasn’t stupidity.

It was compression under asymmetrical knowledge.

If someone showed Nicolas Cage a machine ten thousand years beyond his comprehension, what would he call it?

Quantum something.

Alien technology.

Artificial intelligence.

Dimensional.

God.

Whatever vocabulary was lying nearest to the hole in his understanding.

—

Years later, Nicolas Cage returned.

By then Akwena had changed.

There was a small airstrip.

Solar panels.

A radio tower.

Children studied physics alongside traditional stories.

People knew exactly what airplanes were.

Mostly.

Cage sat with Kito, now an old man.

On the wall of the schoolhouse was an old painting.

It depicted the first aircraft.

The Silver Ancestor.

Beneath it stood a tiny figure wearing black clothing and sunglasses.

Cage pointed.

“Why am I still in this?”

Kito smiled.

“Tradition.”

“But everyone knows I didn’t fall from the airplane.”

“Yes.”

“They know what engines are.”

“Yes.”

“They understand flight.”

“Yes.”

“So why keep the story?”

Kito looked at the painting.

“Because it is not about airplanes anymore.”

Cage went quiet.

“What is it about?”

“The day our world became larger.”

That answer stopped him.

Children ran past them carrying notebooks.

Above, a commercial jet crossed the sky.

Nobody looked up.

Except Cage.

He watched it for a while.

Then he said:

“You know, my people do this too.”

Kito laughed.

“I assumed.”

“No, really. We have machines nobody understands. Markets nobody understands. Algorithms deciding things nobody sees. Invisible signals everywhere.”

“Your Silver Ancestors?”

Cage smiled.

“Something like that.”

“And your priests?”

Cage thought about financial analysts.

Tech CEOs.

Influencers.

Consultants.

People explaining algorithms they had never seen.

He began laughing.

Kito waited.

Cage finally said:

“We have many.”

—

That evening, Cage found several children gathered around his phone.

One had opened an AI assistant.

They were asking it questions.

“What is the largest animal?”

“What is beyond the stars?”

“Can birds remember faces?”

“Why does thunder happen?”

The answers appeared instantly.

One child looked at Cage.

“It knows everything.”

Cage leaned down.

“No.”

The child frowned.

“It answers everything.”

“That is different.”

The child looked back at the screen.

Cage suddenly felt an ancient unease.

A familiar one.

The Silver Ancestor.

The flashlight.

The radio.

The sun cloth.

A thing appears.

It does something astonishing.

Someone asks how.

Nobody truly knows.

So the story begins.

Cage crouched beside the children.

“Here is something important.”

They looked up.

“When something seems magical, don’t stop at naming the magic.”

He pointed toward the phone.

“Ask what makes it work.”

One child asked:

“And if we cannot understand?”

Cage smiled.

“Then make your best story.”

He paused.

“But leave room to change it.”

Kito, sitting nearby, nodded.

“That is better.”

Cage looked over.

“Better than what?”

“Your first explanation.”

“What did I say?”

Kito smiled.

“Lightning sleeping inside stone.”

Cage stared.

“That was you.”

Kito laughed.

Above them, another airplane crossed the evening sky.

This time no one knelt.

A little girl looked up, watched it for three seconds, then returned to her game.

Cage watched her.

For some reason, that moved him more than the first encounter had.

The gods had become machines.

The machines had become ordinary.

And somewhere beyond the edge of everyone’s understanding, new gods were already waiting to be named.

That abstraction is the piece worth keeping: mythology can be treated as a compression layer between observation and explanation. When the explanatory gap is huge, metaphor has to span more distance. As knowledge increases, some myths collapse, some become historical memory, and some survive because they were carrying meaning beyond the literal mechanism in the first place.